<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bandung Conference</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Asian–African Conference</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Asian–African Conference, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> Conference, was a meeting of 29 newly independent and emerging states of Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> held at Bandung, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, in April 1955. Hosted by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and attended by leaders such as Nehru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>, it condemned colonialism and adopted a ten-point declaration on world peace and co-operation. It helped pave the way for the Non-Aligned Movement of 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bandung-conference.jpg" alt="Bandung Conference" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Foreign Ministry of the Republic of Indonesia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plenary_session_during_the_Bandung_Conference.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
